---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const heatCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Quench: freeze the high-T structure', note: 'Heat steel into austenite, then quench (water/oil): carbon has no time to diffuse, and a distorted trapped structure forms — martensite, extremely hard, extremely brittle. A needle-spike microstructure locked by speed (the diagram lesson\'s shortcut).' },
    { math: '2 · Temper: trade hardness for toughness', note: 'Re-warm the quenched part (150–650 °C): some martensite relaxes, brittleness falls, a little hardness goes. Quench-and-temper is the one-two that gives tools their combination — hard enough to cut, tough enough not to shatter.' },
    { math: '3 · Anneal and normalise: soften and refine', note: 'Slow cooling (anneal) maximises softness for forming; air cooling (normalise) refines grains for a uniform machinable structure. The heat-treater\'s dial: cooling rate → microstructure → properties (the defect lesson\'s grain-size lever, applied by oven).' },
    { math: '4 · Case hardening: a hard skin on a tough core', note: 'Carburising (diffuse carbon into the surface — the diffusion lesson, industrially) then quench: gear teeth with hard wears-resistant cases over tough shock-absorbing cores. The surface and the bulk get different jobs — materials design at the component level.' },
  ],
};

const heatQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    { q: 'Carbon diffusing in austenite: raising the furnace from 900 °C to 1000 °C speeds diffusion by roughly…', choices: ['10%', '3×', '100×', 'no change'], answer: 1, why: 'Arrhenius: D = D₀·exp(−Q/RT). For C in γ-iron (Q ≈ 148 kJ/mol) the exponent shifts enough that D roughly triples per 100 °C — which is why case-hardening furnaces fight for every degree.' },
    { q: 'The typical diffusion penetration depth scales as…', choices: ['D·t', '√(D·t)', 't/D', 'exp(D·t)'], answer: 1, why: 'Fickian profiles spread with the error-function solution; the characteristic distance is √(Dt). At 1000 °C, carbon penetrates about 0.27 mm in an hour — case depth is time-budgeted.' },
    { q: 'Martensite is hard because…', choices: ['it is a new equilibrium phase from the diagram', 'quenching traps carbon in a distorted lattice full of obstacles that block dislocations', 'it contains more carbon than before', 'quenching removes impurities'], answer: 1, why: 'The chemistry did not change — carbon atoms locked into interstitial sites strain the lattice into BCT, and that congestion is what dislocations cannot get through. Hardness from trapped disequilibrium, not new composition.' },
    { q: 'Tempering a quenched steel means…', choices: ['cooling it even faster', 're-heating to 150–650 °C to relax martensite — trading some hardness for toughness', 'adding carbon', 'melting the surface'], answer: 1, why: 'Fresh martensite is brittle and full of quench stress. Re-warming lets fine carbides precipitate and strains relax: hardness drops a little, toughness rises a lot. Quench-then-temper is the package deal.' },
  ],
};

const htFickOrder = {
  "prompt": "From one atomic hop to a carburising budget — drag the chain into order:",
  "items": [
    "An atom swaps with a vacancy, or squeezes between lattice sites",
    "Each hop is one step of a random walk — biased down the concentration gradient",
    "The result is a flux proportional to the gradient: J = −D·(dC/dx)",
    "Left alone, the gradient itself smooths with time (Fick's second law)",
    "Its solutions give the working rule: penetration depth grows as √(Dt)"
  ],
  "why": "Quadruple the furnace hold and the case deepens only twofold — every carburising schedule is that square root, priced in hours."
};

const htArrheniusSpot = {
  "prompt": "A process note on diffusivity — one line is wrong. Click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Diffusivity obeys D = D₀·exp(−Q/RT): every hop must clear an energy barrier.",
    "For carbon in austenite, D₀ ≈ 2.3 × 10⁻⁵ m²/s with Q ≈ 148 kJ/mol.",
    "Raising the furnace from 900 °C to 1000 °C roughly triples D.",
    "A quench cools through hundreds of degrees in seconds, cutting diffusion to about half its furnace rate.",
    "That exponential sensitivity is why furnace builders fight over tens of degrees."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "A quench does not halve diffusion — it effectively stops atomic motion mid-flight. Exponential sensitivity cuts both ways: modest heating multiplies hops enormously, and rapid cooling suppresses them almost completely.",
  "why": "The same exponential that makes kilns fight over degrees is what lets a water tank freeze a microstructure in place."
};

const htNosePlace = {
  "prompt": "For eutectoid steel, transformation is fastest at the nose of the C. Place it on the temperature axis:",
  "labels": [
    "100",
    "300",
    "500",
    "700",
    "900"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 100,
    "max": 900
  },
  "answer": 550,
  "tolerance": 50,
  "why": "Near 550 °C. Above it the driving force is small; below it diffusion crawls — the race is only lost in the middle, which is why a quench must outrun that one spot."
};

const htQuenchEstimate = {
  "prompt": "The 0.8 % C shaft ends at 45 HRC after tempering and 20 HRC furnace-cooled. At its hardest — straight out of the water quench, before tempering — roughly what hardness did it touch?",
  "unit": "HRC",
  "answer": 65,
  "factor": 1.25,
  "why": "About 65 HRC: full martensite, carbon trapped in a distorted BCT lattice — and maximum thermal stress, which is why high-carbon grades are the ones that quench-crack. The temper then settles it to 45 HRC.",
  "context": "Same alloy, 20 → 65 → 45 HRC: chemistry never moved, only the itinerary."
};

const htMisconceptionJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "Annealing, normalising and tempering are three names for the same operation — making steel softer.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Anneal = reset to full equilibrium; normalise = air-cooled grain cleanup; temper = partial retreat from martensite. Different itineraries, different targets."
    },
    {
      "s": "Tempering a normalised steel accomplishes nothing, because there is no martensite to relax.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The temper's whole job is relieving trapped martensite — no martensite, no settlement to negotiate."
    },
    {
      "s": "Quenching hardens steel because cooling quickly itself adds strength.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Speed adds nothing on its own. The quench works by preventing diffusion, so metastable martensite can form that equilibrium would never allow."
    },
    {
      "s": "Cool the same steel too slowly and you get soft pearlite no matter how dramatically the water boils.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Only paths that outrun the nose of the C-curve escape to martensite."
    }
  ]
};

const htTemperBuckets = {
  "prompt": "The spec sheet's T-temper is an ageing recipe — file each condition:",
  "buckets": [
    "O — annealed",
    "T4 — naturally aged",
    "T6 — artificially aged"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "2024 as delivered, ~75 MPa yield",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "solution-treated, quenched, then held at room temperature",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "2024-T4, ~325 MPa yield",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "aged warm at 150–190 °C after solutionising",
      "bucket": 2
    },
    {
      "label": "7075-T6, exceeding 500 MPa yield",
      "bucket": 2
    },
    {
      "label": "GP zones and θ″/θ′ plates at peak spacing",
      "bucket": 2
    }
  ],
  "why": "T4 means solution-treated and naturally aged; T6 means solution-treated and artificially aged. Hold too long or too hot past either peak and the precipitates coarsen — strength falls again."
};

const htCaseMatch = {
  "prompt": "Four ways to harden only the surface — pair each with its mechanism:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "carburising",
      "right": "carbon seeps inward at ~900–950 °C, then quench-and-temper hardens only the carbon-rich skin"
    },
    {
      "left": "nitriding",
      "right": "nitrogen at ~500 °C, below transformation temperature — minimal distortion, for precision work"
    },
    {
      "left": "flame / induction hardening",
      "right": "no chemistry at all — heat only the surface past transformation, quench before the core notices"
    },
    {
      "left": "the clay-coated blade",
      "right": "thick insulation on the spine, bare steel at the edge — hard edge, tough spine, visible hamon"
    }
  ],
  "why": "All four give surface and bulk different jobs: a wear-resistant case over a shock-absorbing core. The Japanese smiths ran the experiment centuries before the vocabulary existed."
};

const htQuenchBranch = {
  "root": "The violence budget: every quench is thermal shock, and half of heat-treatment engineering moderates the other half.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "A batch of high-carbon parts keeps quench-cracking in plain water — which cools fast through the nose and fast again exactly where martensite forms. What do you change?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Switch to a quenchant tuned: fast through the nose, slow below martensite start",
          "next": "tuned",
          "feedback": "Oil, salt and polymer quenchants exist precisely to run that order — the reverse of plain water."
        },
        {
          "label": "Interrupt the quench — pull the part out above martensite start and hold",
          "next": "interrupted",
          "feedback": "Austempering and martempering: bend the schedule instead of the part."
        },
        {
          "label": "Quench harder in brine to get it over with",
          "next": "brine",
          "feedback": "Faster through the nose — and faster again through martensite start, exactly where the cracking lives."
        }
      ]
    },
    "tuned": {
      "text": "A tuned quenchant races past the nose (where pearlite waits) then cools gently past martensite start. Press quenching holds geometry; a cryogenic trip below finishes retained austenite on purpose.",
      "choices": []
    },
    "interrupted": {
      "text": "Held above martensite start, the section equalises before transformation begins — thermal gradients that would have cracked the part never get built. The transformation then runs at low stress.",
      "choices": []
    },
    "brine": {
      "text": "The fastest route to full martensite is also the fastest route to quench cracks: the surface contracts around a hot core, and the stresses warp or split the part. Speed was never the cure here.",
      "choices": []
    }
  },
  "why": "Cooling rate is a curve to be shaped, not a number to maximise — fast where the nose waits, gentle where martensite forms."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/advanced/heat-treatment">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Materials Science lesson. It assumes the foundational lesson <em>Why
    Materials Behave</em>, the intermediate lessons <em>Atoms to Crystals</em> and <em>Metals,
    Ceramics, Polymers, Composites</em>, and the advanced <em>Phase Diagrams</em> lesson, which
    supplied the equilibrium map this page now escapes. You need that map's vocabulary and one
    physics idea — diffusion — that this page builds from scratch.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state Fick's laws and use the square-root-of-D·t penetration rule to budget a case depth;</li>
    <li>predict how strongly diffusivity responds to temperature from the Arrhenius law;</li>
    <li>read a TTT diagram and trace anneal, normalise, quench and temper as paths across it;</li>
    <li>explain martensite as trapped disequilibrium — and why tempering trades hardness for toughness;</li>
    <li>describe precipitation hardening and case hardening as scheduled obstacle fields, and name how each fails.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Heat treatment is <strong>microstructure by itinerary</strong>: identical chemistry, a
    different temperature–time path, a different material. A eutectoid steel can be soft
    enough to machine or hard enough to cut glass depending on nothing but how fast it was
    cooled from 800 °C. The blacksmith's hiss of a blade entering the quench tank was this
    lesson in empirical form; the phase-diagram lesson supplied the map, and this lesson adds
    the physics the map leaves out — <strong>time</strong>. Two forces set every treatment:
    thermodynamics says which state the atoms <em>want</em>, and diffusion sets how fast they
    can <em>get there</em>. Heat treatment is the deliberate engineering of the race between
    them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="quench, temper, anneal, case-harden" widget="equationStep" config={heatCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four cards are four itineraries, not four ingredients —
    every one starts from heat and ends at a different cooling schedule. Quench and temper work
    as a package (card 2 only makes sense after card 1); anneal and normalise are slower paths to
    softer destinations; case hardening adds a composition gradient before the quench. Keep the
    dial in view: cooling rate, microstructure, properties — in that order every time.
  </p>

  <h2>Diffusion I: the mechanism and Fick's laws</h2>
  <p>
    Atoms in a crystal move by <strong>hopping</strong>: an atom swaps places with a vacancy
    (substitutional diffusion — slow, needs vacancies) or squeezes between lattice sites
    (interstitial diffusion — the carbon-in-iron case, much faster because carbon is tiny).
    Either way motion is a biased random walk down the concentration gradient, which Fick
    wrote as a law: <strong>flux J = −D·(dC/dx)</strong> — atoms flow from where they are
    concentrated to where they are not, at a rate proportional to the gradient, scaled by the
    <strong>diffusivity</strong> D. The unsteady version, Fick's second law, describes the
    gradient itself smoothing over time, and its solutions carry the field's most useful
    rule of thumb: the characteristic penetration depth grows as <strong>√(Dt)</strong>.
    Quadruple the time and the case deepens only twofold; the arithmetic of every
    carburising schedule follows from that square root.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild the chain from a single hop to a design rule" widget="order" config={htFickOrder} />
  <p>
    D itself obeys an Arrhenius law, D = D₀·exp(−Q/RT), because every hop must clear an
    energy barrier. Worked example, carbon in FCC iron (austenite): D₀ ≈ 2.3 × 10⁻⁵ m²/s
    with activation energy Q ≈ 148 kJ/mol. At 900 °C (1173 K) the exponential gives
    D ≈ 5.9 × 10⁻¹² m²/s; at 1000 °C (1273 K), D ≈ 1.9 × 10⁻¹¹ m²/s — a hundred-degree
    furnace change buys a <strong>3× speed-up</strong>. This is the tyranny and the gift of
    the exponential: diffusion is pathologically temperature-sensitive, so kilns compete
    over tens of degrees, and the same sensitivity is why a quench (cool through hundreds
    of degrees in seconds) can effectively stop atomic motion mid-flight.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="one line of the furnace note misreads the exponential" widget="spot-error" config={htArrheniusSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> atoms hop, the hops obey Fick's laws, and the hop rate is
    exponentially temperature-sensitive — which is why furnaces fight over tens of degrees.
    Next, the tool that turns this physics into a treatment plan: a map of what happens when,
    drawn for one alloy at a time.
  </p>

  <h2>Diffusion II: time–temperature and the TTT map</h2>
  <p>
    The equilibrium phase diagram knows nothing of time; the fix is the
    <strong>TTT (time–temperature–transformation) diagram</strong>, drawn for one alloy: for
    each temperature below the austenite field, a curve marks when transformation
    <em>begins</em> and when it <em>finishes</em>. The curve is C-shaped: at high temperature
    transformation is slow because the driving force is small; at low temperature it is slow
    because diffusion is; the fastest transformation sits in the nose of the C (~550 °C for
    eutectoid steel). Every treatment is a path drawn on this map. <strong>Anneal</strong>:
    cool slowly down the right side — diffusion keeps pace, coarse <strong>pearlite</strong>
    forms, maximum softness. <strong>Normalise</strong>: cool faster in air — finer
    pearlite, uniform grain, a machinable compromise. <strong>Quench</strong>: cool faster
    than the nose can catch — you miss every equilibrium product and land in a regime the
    equilibrium diagram does not contain: <strong>martensite</strong>, carbon trapped in a
    distorted BCT lattice (the FCC-to-BCC transition happens without diffusion, and the
    trapped interstitials strain it), extremely hard (about 65 HRC) and dangerously brittle.
    <strong>Temper</strong> finishes the job: re-warming to 150–650 °C lets carbon escape
    into fine carbides and stresses relax — hardness falls a little (to, say, 55–60 HRC),
    toughness rises enormously. The classic schedule — quench, then temper — is a
    thermodynamic cheat followed by a negotiated settlement. A companion diagram, the
    <strong>CCT</strong> (continuous-cooling) version, adapts TTT to real furnace ramps;
    and the <strong>Jominy end-quench test</strong> measures a grade's
    <em>hardenability</em> — how deep below the surface a quench can still outrun the nose,
    which is alloying's real service: boron or chromium shift the C-curve right, buying the
    quench more time.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="put the nose of the C-curve on the temperature axis" widget="place" config={htNosePlace} />
  <p>
    Follow one part through the whole map. Take a 0.8 % carbon (eutectoid) steel shaft,
    25 mm across. Austenitise at 800 °C for half an hour — long enough that the √(Dt) rule
    has carried carbon homogeneously through the section. Quench in agitated water: the
    surface plunges past the nose in seconds, misses every equilibrium product, and
    transforms below its martensite-start into full martensite at roughly 65 HRC — though
    it does so while carrying maximum thermal stress, which is why high-carbon grades are
    the ones that quench-crack. Temper at 400 °C for an hour: hardness settles near 45 HRC
    and the part is spring-tough. The identical steel, furnace-cooled instead, arrives at
    coarse pearlite around 20 HRC. Same alloy, a 45-point hardness spread, chosen entirely
    by itinerary — and note what the example does <em>not</em> involve: no composition
    change, no new phase from the diagram, nothing but path.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the quench's peak hardness before the temper" widget="estimate" config={htQuenchEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the TTT map turns cooling rate into destiny — slow gives soft
    pearlite, fast traps martensite — and tempering settles the quenched structure into
    something usable. Next, a slower race run on purpose: precipitation hardening, where the
    obstacle field is scheduled rather than trapped.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> annealing, normalising and tempering are three words
    for the same thing — "making it softer". They are different itineraries with different
    targets. Annealing is a <em>reset</em>: full equilibrium, maximum softness, for forming
    operations. Normalising is a <em>cleanup</em>: air cooling to erase a prior
    treatment's coarse or non-uniform grain. Tempering is a <em>partial retreat</em>:
    it starts from martensite and gives back just enough hardness to buy toughness. Run
    the wrong one and the part fails its job — temper a normalized steel and nothing
    changes, because there is no martensite to relax.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — three itineraries, one vocabulary audit" widget="judge" config={htMisconceptionJudge} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> quenching makes steel hard because it "cools it fast" or
    "freezes it strong". Nothing about speed itself adds strength. The quench works by
    <em>preventing diffusion</em> — it is the mechanical suppression of Fick's law — so that
    a metastable structure (martensite) forms that equilibrium would never allow. Cool the
    same steel too slowly and you get soft pearlite no matter how dramatically the water
    boils.
  </p>

  <h2>Precipitation hardening: ageing on purpose</h2>
  <p>
    The aerospace heat treatment is a slower race, run deliberately. In the Al–Cu system
    (2024 aluminium), copper dissolves plentifully at ~540 °C but almost not at all at room
    temperature. The schedule: heat to dissolve (solution treat), quench to trap a
    <strong>supersaturated solid solution</strong>, then <strong>age</strong> at 150–190 °C —
    warm enough for copper to migrate, cool enough that equilibrium is still far away. The
    copper precipitates as nanoscale clusters (Guinier–Preston zones, then metastable θ″/θ′
    plates) spaced tens of nanometres apart — precisely the obstacle field dislocations
    cannot slice through cheaply. The numbers are dramatic: 2024 in the annealed O
    condition yields about 75 MPa; after solution treatment and natural ageing to T4 it
    yields around 325 MPa — more than fourfold — and artificially aged 7075-T6 exceeds
    500 MPa. The airframe's strength is a scheduled precipitation, which is why the spec
    sheet's T-temper is really an ageing recipe: T4 means solution-treated and naturally
    aged, T6 means solution-treated and artificially aged. The same physics strengthens
    nickel superalloys (γ′ precipitates) and the newest high-strength steels. The delicate
    part is <strong>overageing</strong>: hold too long or
    too hot and the precipitates coarsen (big ones eat small ones — Ostwald ripening),
    spacing widens, strength falls. Coarsening follows the Lifshitz–Slyozov–Wagner law:
    mean particle radius grows as t^(1/3) at fixed temperature, so doubling a precipitate's
    radius takes eight times the hold — slow enough to catch in process control, but the
    rate constant itself carries an exponential temperature dependence, which is why a few
    degrees too hot ages an alloy faster than a few hours too long ever could. Ageing is a
    thermostat-and-calendar operation with a peak, and quality control is the discipline
    of hitting the peak.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="file each airframe condition by its ageing recipe" widget="sort-buckets" config={htTemperBuckets} />

  <h2>Case hardening: diffusion as a design tool</h2>
  <p>
    A gear tooth wants impossible properties at once — hard, wear-resistant surface;
    tough, shock-absorbing core. Diffusion resolves the contradiction by giving the part a
    composition <em>gradient</em>: <strong>carburising</strong> bakes low-carbon steel in a
    carbon-rich atmosphere at ~900–950 °C, so carbon seeps inward, then quench-and-temper
    hardens only the carbon-rich skin. Quantify with the rule above: carbon in austenite at
    950 °C has D ≈ 1.1 × 10⁻¹¹ m²/s (the same Arrhenius line as before), so an hour gives
    √(Dt) ≈ 0.2 mm of case; a ten-hour soak reaches roughly 0.6 mm — case depth is literally
    a time bill. Variants: <strong>nitriding</strong>
    (nitrogen at ~500 °C, below transformation temperature, minimal distortion — used where
    precision survives) and <strong>flame/induction hardening</strong> (no chemistry — heat
    only the surface past transformation and quench it before the core notices). Differential
    quenching is ancient: the Japanese smith's clay-coated blade — thick insulation on the
    spine, bare steel at the edge — produced hard edge, tough spine and the visible hamon
    line, centuries before TTT diagrams explained why it worked.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="pair each surface treatment with its signature" widget="match" config={htCaseMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "hardening a part means hardening all of it." Heat treatment
  sounds like a whole-part operation — the part goes into the furnace, so the whole part changes.
  But several of the most important treatments act on the surface only, and that is their design
  point, not a compromise: carburising builds a composition <em>gradient</em>, so the quench
  hardens only the carbon-rich skin; flame and induction hardening heat the surface past
  transformation before the core notices anything. The correct picture is the gear tooth this
  section opened with — surface and bulk deliberately given different jobs: a hard,
  wear-resistant case over a tough, shock-absorbing core. The Japanese smith's clay-coated blade
  is the same answer without the vocabulary: one steel, two microstructures, split by insulation.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: state the characteristic penetration-depth rule for diffusion, and say what it implies for doubling a case depth.</li>
    <li>Application: a 0.8% carbon eutectoid steel shaft is austenitised at 800 °C, quenched in agitated water, then tempered at 400 °C for an hour. Give the final hardness and structure, and say why the water quench alone was the risky step.</li>
    <li>Why does adding boron or chromium improve hardenability even though it leaves the equilibrium phases unchanged?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Penetration depth grows as the square root of D times t, so
    doubling a case depth costs four times the hold — case depth is a time bill paid on a square
    root. (2) About 45 HRC, tempered martensite: the quench trapped full martensite near 65 HRC
    under maximum thermal stress — the step that quench-cracks high-carbon grades — and the
    temper relaxed it, trading roughly 20 points of hardness for spring-toughness. (3) Alloying
    shifts the C-curve to the right on the TTT diagram: the nose takes longer to catch any
    cooling path, so the quench has more time to outrun diffusion and reach martensite even deep
    below the surface. Nothing about the equilibrium diagram moved; the kinetics did.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Heat treatment is microstructure by itinerary: identical chemistry, a different temperature–time path, a different material.</li>
    <li>Fick's laws govern the hops; penetration depth grows as the square root of D·t; D itself is Arrhenius and pathologically temperature-sensitive.</li>
    <li>The TTT diagram's C-shape encodes the race: every treatment is a path drawn across it.</li>
    <li>Martensite is carbon trapped in a distorted BCT lattice — hardness from disequilibrium, not from the equilibrium diagram.</li>
    <li>Tempering is a negotiated settlement: a little hardness returned for a lot of toughness.</li>
    <li>Precipitation hardening schedules a nanoscale obstacle field; overageing coarsens it and strength falls.</li>
    <li>Case hardening gives surface and bulk different jobs through a composition gradient.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The advanced sibling <em>Phase Diagrams</em> supplies the thermodynamic half of the race;
    read together they are the full map. <em>Failure:
    Fracture &amp; Fatigue</em> shows what happens when the itinerary is wrong — brittle
    untempered martensite, quench cracks, the flaw-growth physics the violence
    budget sets up.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The phase transformations underneath.</strong> Pearlite, bainite and martensite
    are not phases but <em>transformation products</em> with distinct mechanisms: pearlite
    grows by coupled diffusion (ferrite and cementite lamellae advancing together — the
    eutectoid's cooperative front); bainite is a mid-temperature hybrid (shear plus
    carbon partitioning); martensite is pure shear — a military, diffusionless transformation
    where the lattice distorts in a coordinated wave, which is why it can outrun any cooling
    rate and why each martensite plate forms in microseconds. Transformation
    <strong>nucleation and growth</strong> theory (nucleation barrier ∝ interface energy
    over driving force; growth ∝ diffusion) unifies all of it, and explains the TTT curve's
    shape quantitatively. Homogeneous nucleation of a new phase inside a parent grain is so
    penalised by interface energy that real transformations lean on defects — grain
    boundaries, dislocations — as nucleation sites; the defect lesson's "flaws" turn out to
    be the transformation's doorways.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The violence budget.</strong> Quenches are thermal shock: surface contracts
    around a hot core, generating stresses that warp and crack parts. Half of heat-treatment
    engineering moderates the other half — oil, salt and polymer quenchants with tuned
    cooling curves (fast through the nose, slow through martensite start, which is exactly
    the wrong order for plain water), interrupted quenching (austempering, martempering),
    press quenching to hold geometry, and cryogenic treatment to finish transforming retained
    austenite. Additive manufacturing has made this budget central: 3-D-printed metals
    experience thousands of micro-weld thermal cycles, leaving residual stress and mixed
    microstructures that post-treatments (stress relief, hot isostatic pressing to close
    pores, full re-solutionising) must reconcile — process and heat treatment are now one
    design space, not two.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="walk the quenchant decision" widget="branch" config={htQuenchBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>Tempering, quantified.</strong> Tempering is a sequence of staged reactions,
    not one softening event: stage one (about 100–250 °C) precipitates the transition
    carbide ε (roughly Fe<sub>2.4</sub>C) out of supersaturated martensite; stage two
    (200–300 °C) decomposes any retained austenite; stage three (250–400 °C) replaces ε
    with cementite and lets the dislocation cell structure recover; stage four, at the
    highest temperatures, recrystallises the ferrite matrix. Because hardness depends on
    all of these together, engineers compress the whole schedule into the
    <strong>Hollomon–Jaffe parameter</strong>, HJP = T·(C + log t) with T in kelvin, t in
    hours and C ≈ 20 for plain-carbon steels: any time–temperature pairs with equal HJP
    deliver equal hardness. One hour at 300 °C scores 573 × 20 ≈ 11 500; matching that at
    250 °C requires 523 × (20 + log t) = 11 500, i.e. about 80 hours — the mathematical
    form of the shop-floor rule that each extra 25 °C of tempering temperature is worth
    many extra hours. Two ambushes live inside the tempering range:
    <strong>tempered-martensite embrittlement</strong> near 300–350 °C, where cementite
    films form on lath boundaries, and (reversible) <strong>temper embrittlement</strong>
    at 375–575 °C, where phosphorus, tin and antimony segregate to prior-austenite grain
    boundaries — cured by rapid cooling through the range or by molybdenum additions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where martensite starts.</strong> The martensite-start temperature M<sub>s</sub>
    falls almost linearly with alloy content; the classic Andrews regression,
    M<sub>s</sub> (°C) ≈ 539 − 423·(%C) − 30.4·(%Mn) − 17.7·(%Ni) − 12.1·(%Cr) − 7.5·(%Mo),
    is the workhorse. For the eutectoid 0.8 % C steel it predicts about 200 °C, so such a
    steel only finishes transforming as it nears room temperature. Below M<sub>s</sub> the
    transformed fraction rises with undercooling roughly as the Koistinen–Marburger
    relation, f = exp(−0.011·(M<sub>s</sub> − T)); austenite that survives to room
    temperature is <em>retained austenite</em> — soft, and liable to transform slowly in
    service, wrecking the dimensional stability of gauges and bearings. Precision parts
    therefore take a cryogenic trip to −80 °C or below (cold treatment with liquid
    nitrogen) followed by a further temper, closing the transformation on purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Predicting martensite-start temperatures and quench
    distortion from first principles remains partly empirical — distortion simulation is
    good but not perfect, and "safcracking" (quench cracks in high-carbon grades) still
    surprises. Diffusion data at the low temperatures of battery electrodes and solder joints
    is thin but increasingly load-bearing. And the newest frontier is <em>processing
    without melting</em>: solid-state additive routes and severe plastic deformation create
    microstructures no furnace itinerary can, challenging the assumption that heat treatment
    is the only way to reset a metal's internal state. The race between thermodynamics and
    kinetics still has unexplored tracks.
  </p>

